{% doc %}
  blocks/_comparison-table.liquid — private.

  The table itself: one CSS grid, and every column inside it a `subgrid`.

  ## Why the rows are the grid's and not each column's

  A comparison is only a comparison while *Third-party lab tested* is on the
  same line in all four columns. Four independent columns cannot promise that —
  each is as tall as its own content, so the first claim that wraps to two lines
  in one column and not another slides every row under it out of step, and the
  table goes on looking plausible while saying the wrong thing about three
  products.

  So the rows belong to this block. It declares them once —

      grid-template-rows: var(--ct-head)
                          repeat(var(--ct-rows), minmax(var(--ct-row-min), auto))
                          var(--ct-foot);

  — and each **Comparison column** is a grid item spanning all of them with
  `grid-template-rows: subgrid`, so its cells sit in *these* tracks rather than
  in tracks of their own. A claim that wraps grows its row in every column at
  once, at the moment it wraps, with nothing measured in JavaScript and no fixed
  heights anywhere.

  `@supports not (grid-template-rows: subgrid)` falls back to fixed row heights,
  which is the drawn design exactly — 70px rows — and only loses the ability to
  grow.

  ## Feature rows is a number because Liquid cannot count blocks

  `block.blocks` does not exist: a block cannot read its children, and
  `forloop` does not reach inside `{% content_for 'blocks' %}`. The row track
  list needs a count before any child renders, so the count is a setting.

  `assets/comparison-table.js` corrects it on connect from the number of cells
  actually in the first column, so a merchant who adds a seventh row and forgets
  the slider sees the right table anyway. The setting is what makes the page
  correct *before* and *without* that script, which is why it is not simply left
  to JavaScript.

  ## Why the chooser lives here and not on each column

  Every alternative column in the drawing offers the same list — because the
  list *is* the set of alternative columns. Each column drawing its own copy
  would mean the merchant typing every name once per column and keeping four
  lists in step by hand, which is the trap `blocks/_featured-pills-tabs.liquid`
  documents at length.

  So each column publishes its own name on `data-comparison-name`, and
  `assets/comparison-table.js` builds every chooser's list from all of them —
  the same shape as `<tab-group>` building its buttons from its panels. How the
  chooser *looks* is set here too, once, so all of them agree.

  With the script absent every alternative column renders side by side, named,
  complete and readable. What is lost is the switching, not the content.

  ## Alternatives shown, per device

  The drawing has two alternatives beside the brand on a wide screen. A 390px
  phone cannot hold four columns of text, so the count is a setting per device
  and the columns past it are hidden rather than squeezed — the chooser is how
  the customer reaches the rest, which is the same job it does on a desktop.

  Private — the leading underscore — because a table of comparison columns is
  not something to drop into a footer. It is addable inside
  `sections/comparison-table.liquid` and nowhere else.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  comment
    ---- the columns ----

    The label column is wider than the product columns — 312 against 253 in the
    drawing — so it is expressed as a ratio of one product column rather than as
    a width. A ratio survives every container width and every column count; a
    width does not.

    It reaches CSS as a bare `fr`, computed here, because `calc(1.23 * 1fr)` is
    not something to rely on across browsers and `repeat()` will not take a
    `calc()` as its count either. Liquid does the arithmetic; CSS receives
    literals.
  endcomment
  assign label_ratio = s.label_ratio | default: 123 | plus: 0
  assign label_col = label_ratio | divided_by: 100.0

  assign label_ratio_tablet = s.label_ratio_tablet | default: label_ratio | plus: 0
  assign label_col_tablet = label_ratio_tablet | divided_by: 100.0

  assign label_ratio_mobile = s.label_ratio_mobile | default: label_ratio_tablet | plus: 0
  assign label_col_mobile = label_ratio_mobile | divided_by: 100.0

  comment
    One brand column plus however many alternatives that device shows. Summed
    here rather than in a `calc()` inside `repeat()`, for the reason above.

    A merchant who has added fewer alternative columns than the count asks for
    would leave an empty track; `assets/comparison-table.js` writes the real
    number over these three the moment it connects.
  endcomment
  assign slots = s.slots | default: 2 | plus: 0
  assign slots_tablet = s.slots_tablet | default: 1 | plus: 0
  assign slots_mobile = s.slots_mobile | default: 1 | plus: 0

  assign cols = slots | plus: 1
  assign cols_tablet = slots_tablet | plus: 1
  assign cols_mobile = slots_mobile | plus: 1

  assign rows = s.rows | default: 6 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the chooser ----

    Resolved the way every border in this theme is: `none` emits nothing at all,
    and with no colour picked the line is `currentColor` faded by `color-mix`,
    because the colour filters cannot take an alpha channel from a keyword.
  endcomment
  assign trigger_line = s.trigger_border_style | default: 'none'
  assign trigger_width = 0
  if trigger_line != 'none'
    assign trigger_width = s.trigger_border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
    assign trigger_percent = s.trigger_border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if s.trigger_border_color == blank
      assign trigger_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: trigger_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign trigger_alpha = trigger_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign trigger_paint = s.trigger_border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', trigger_alpha
    endif
  endif

  assign panel_line = s.panel_border_style | default: 'none'
  assign panel_width = 0
  if panel_line != 'none'
    assign panel_width = s.panel_border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
    assign panel_percent = s.panel_border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if s.panel_border_color == blank
      assign panel_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: panel_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign panel_alpha = panel_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign panel_paint = s.panel_border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', panel_alpha
    endif
  endif

  assign table_line = s.border_style | default: 'none'
  assign table_width = 0
  if table_line != 'none'
    assign table_width = s.border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
    assign table_percent = s.border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if s.border_color == blank
      assign table_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: table_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign table_alpha = table_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign table_paint = s.border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', table_alpha
    endif
  endif

  assign label_text = s.accessible_label | default: 'Product comparison'
-%}

{%- capture table_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --ct-label-col: {{ label_col }}fr;
  --ct-label-col-tablet: {{ label_col_tablet }}fr;
  --ct-label-col-mobile: {{ label_col_mobile }}fr;
  --ct-cols: {{ cols }};
  --ct-cols-tablet: {{ cols_tablet }};
  --ct-cols-mobile: {{ cols_mobile }};
  --ct-slots: {{ slots }};
  --ct-slots-tablet: {{ slots_tablet }};
  --ct-slots-mobile: {{ slots_mobile }};
  --ct-rows: {{ rows }};
  --ct-gap: {{ s.column_gap | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-gap-mobile: {{ s.column_gap_mobile | default: 6 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-head: {{ s.head_height | default: 130 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-head-mobile: {{ s.head_height_mobile | default: 88 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-row-min: {{ s.row_height | default: 70 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-row-min-mobile: {{ s.row_height_mobile | default: 56 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-foot: {{ s.foot_height | default: 32 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-foot-mobile: {{ s.foot_height_mobile | default: 20 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-radius: {{ s.border_radius | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-border-width: {{ table_width }}px;
  {%- if table_width > 0 -%}
    --ct-border-style: {{ table_line }};
    --ct-border-color: {{ table_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.background != blank -%}
    --ct-background: {{ s.background }};
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the chooser, set once for every column ----
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --ct-trigger-radius: {{ s.trigger_radius | default: 24 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-trigger-pad-block: {{ s.trigger_padding_block | default: 13 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-trigger-pad-inline: {{ s.trigger_padding_inline | default: 14 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-trigger-size: {{ s.trigger_font_size | default: 16 | plus: 0 | divided_by: 10.0 }}rem;
  --ct-trigger-size-mobile: {{ s.trigger_font_size_mobile | default: 12 | plus: 0 | divided_by: 10.0 }}rem;
  --ct-trigger-weight: {{ s.trigger_font_weight | default: '700' }};
  --ct-trigger-gap: {{ s.trigger_gap | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-trigger-icon: {{ s.trigger_icon_size | default: 14 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-trigger-border-width: {{ trigger_width }}px;
  {%- if trigger_width > 0 -%}
    --ct-trigger-border-style: {{ trigger_line }};
    --ct-trigger-border-color: {{ trigger_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.trigger_background != blank -%}
    --ct-trigger-background: {{ s.trigger_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.trigger_color != blank -%}
    --ct-trigger-color: {{ s.trigger_color }};
  {%- endif -%}

  --ct-panel-radius: {{ s.panel_radius | default: 12 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-panel-pad-block: {{ s.panel_padding_block | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-panel-pad-inline: {{ s.panel_padding_inline | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-panel-offset: {{ s.panel_offset | default: 4 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-panel-border-width: {{ panel_width }}px;
  {%- if panel_width > 0 -%}
    --ct-panel-border-style: {{ panel_line }};
    --ct-panel-border-color: {{ panel_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.panel_background != blank -%}
    --ct-panel-background: {{ s.panel_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    One checkbox, not four sliders.

    Distance, blur and colour were three more controls and three more
    translation keys for a value no merchant tunes, on a file that is at its
    key ceiling. The shadow is `currentColor` faded rather than a fixed dark,
    so it follows the colour scheme the table is sitting in instead of holding
    one colour across all of them — the same reasoning every border in this
    theme uses when no colour is picked.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.panel_shadow -%}
    --ct-panel-shadow: 0 12px 32px color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 12%, transparent);
  {%- else -%}
    --ct-panel-shadow: none;
  {%- endif -%}

  --ct-option-size: {{ s.option_font_size | default: 16 | plus: 0 | divided_by: 10.0 }}rem;
  --ct-option-size-mobile: {{ s.option_font_size_mobile | default: 14 | plus: 0 | divided_by: 10.0 }}rem;
  --ct-option-pad-block: {{ s.option_padding_block | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-option-pad-inline: {{ s.option_padding_inline | default: 16 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-option-radius: {{ s.option_radius | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- if s.option_color != blank -%}
    --ct-option-color: {{ s.option_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.option_active_color != blank -%}
    --ct-option-active-color: {{ s.option_active_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.option_active_background != blank -%}
    --ct-option-active-background: {{ s.option_active_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  --ct-option-active-weight: {{ s.option_active_weight | default: '700' }};

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the two marks ----

    A tick and a cross are the same element with two palettes, and both are set
    here rather than on each cell for the reason the chooser is: a table whose
    third tick is a different green from its second is a table that will be.
    Each cell picks a palette by name — `var(--ct-mark-yes-fill)` or
    `var(--ct-mark-no-fill)` — so a cell that overrides nothing emits nothing.

    A fill of `none` rather than a colour is what makes the cross an outline:
    both palettes carry the same six properties, and the difference between a
    disc and a ring is which of them the merchant filled in.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --ct-mark-yes-size: {{ s.mark_yes_size | default: 24 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-yes-size-mobile: {{ s.mark_yes_size_mobile | default: 20 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-yes-icon-size: {{ s.mark_yes_icon_size | default: 12 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-yes-fill: {% if s.mark_yes_fill != blank %}{{ s.mark_yes_fill }}{% else %}none{% endif %};
  --ct-mark-yes-ring-width: {{ s.mark_yes_ring_width | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-yes-ring-color: {{ s.mark_yes_ring_color | default: 'transparent' }};
  --ct-mark-yes-color: {{ s.mark_yes_color | default: '#161514' }};

  --ct-mark-no-size: {{ s.mark_no_size | default: 20 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-no-size-mobile: {{ s.mark_no_size_mobile | default: 18 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-no-icon-size: {{ s.mark_no_icon_size | default: 9 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-no-fill: {% if s.mark_no_fill != blank %}{{ s.mark_no_fill }}{% else %}none{% endif %};
  --ct-mark-no-ring-width: {{ s.mark_no_ring_width | default: 1 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --ct-mark-no-ring-color: {{ s.mark_no_ring_color | default: '#03D26F' }};
  --ct-mark-no-color: {{ s.mark_no_color | default: '#161514' }};
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `<comparison-table>` is the grid. Making the custom element the grid rather
  than a wrapper around one keeps the columns as its direct children, which is
  what `subgrid` needs — an element in between would be a grid item with tracks
  of its own and the alignment this block exists for would be gone.

  It is not `role="table"`. The DOM here is column-major and the visual rows are
  grid tracks, so a `table` role would be a promise about row order that the
  markup cannot keep. It is a labelled group instead, each column is a labelled
  group inside it, and `assets/comparison-table.js` names every mark with the
  claim it answers — which it can do, because it knows each cell's position and
  Liquid does not.
{%- endcomment -%}
<comparison-table
  class="comparison-grid"
  data-block-spacing
  data-rows="{{ rows }}"
  data-slots="{{ slots }}"
  data-slots-tablet="{{ slots_tablet }}"
  data-slots-mobile="{{ slots_mobile }}"
  role="group"
  aria-label="{{ label_text | escape }}"
  style="{{ table_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {% content_for 'blocks' %}
</comparison-table>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% # theme-check-disable ExcessiveSettingsCount %}
